@misc{Feng_Guiding_LLMs_Engineering_Design_Process_Domain_Specific_Knowledge_2025,
  title = {Guiding Large Language Models (Llms) through the Engineering Design Process Using Domain-Specific Knowledge Bases},
  author = {Feng, Wangchuan Bradley and Szajnfarber, Zoe and Panchal, Jitesh H.},
  year = 2025,
  month = sep,
  howpublished = {AI4SE \& SE4AI Workshop 2025 presentation},
  note = {Insights: Demonstrates that constraining LLMs with domain-specific knowledge graphs derived from engineering patents preserves system structure, constraints, and functional relationships during the design synthesis process. \textbar\textbar{} TL;DR: This presentation shows that providing LLMs with structured, patent-derived knowledge graphs significantly improves engineering design outcomes compared to unconstrained or generic RAG approaches. \textbar\textbar{} Contributions: - Identifies key systems engineering challenges in LLM-based design, including loss of constraints, relationships, and design iteration knowledge (p. 3). - Proposes a knowledge-graph-guided design pipeline combining RAG, GraphRAG, and clustered patent knowledge (pp. 4--7). - Introduces a four-step methodology: patent selection via CPC codes, per-patent knowledge graph construction, graph clustering using TF-IDF, and LLM-guided design synthesis (pp. 9--13). - Demonstrates cross-domain design transfer by adapting agricultural and semiconductor gripper patents to a robotic can-gripper design task (pp. 14--16). - Shows that hierarchical knowledge graphs reduce LLM context length while increasing solution diversity and design coherence (p. 17). \textbar\textbar{} Challenges: - Current approach does not fully encode inter-function constraints and trade relationships. - Knowledge graph construction quality depends on patent text clarity and extraction accuracy. - Automatic CPC code selection from problem statements remains an open problem. - Scaling to very large or highly novel design spaces requires further validation.
\par
Presented September 18, 2025, Washington, DC. Purdue University and The George Washington University. Focuses on guiding LLM-based engineering design using patent-derived knowledge graphs},
