  note = {Insights: Demonstrates that multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation (M-RAG) combined with frontier LLMs can significantly improve both the generation and evaluation of system design documents when guided by system-engineering-specific criteria. \textbar\textbar{} TL;DR: This paper introduces the SE Assistant, a GPT-4-based M-RAG system that automates generation and evaluation of system design documents, outperforming baseline GPT-4 and text-only RAG approaches. \textbar\textbar{} Contributions: - Introduces the SE Assistant, a generative AI platform for producing and evaluating system Design Documents (DesDocs) from system requirements (pp. 2--3). - Proposes a multimodal RAG (M-RAG) pipeline that incorporates text, images, and tables to provide design-relevant context to GPT-4 (Fig. 1). - Defines a structured, design-specific evaluation methodology using multiple LLMs (GPT-4, Claude-2, Gemini) combined with weighted scoring and human engineer review (pp. 3--4). - Demonstrates empirical improvements in requirements coverage and evaluation scores using M-RAG versus GPT-4-only and text-only RAG methods (Table I). - Frames LLM-based evaluation as a scalable alternative to purely human-led system design validation. \textbar\textbar{} Challenges: - Evaluation remains partially subjective despite structured criteria and multi-LLM scoring. - Dependence on frontier proprietary models limits reproducibility and deployment flexibility. - Physical feasibility and downstream implementation risks are not fully validated. - Scaling multimodal context ingestion and governance across large engineering programs remains open.},
