  howpublished = {AI4SE \& SE4AI Workshop 2025 presentation},
  note = {Insights: Positions technical debt as an explicit, engineerable risk in aerospace test and evaluation and shows that LLM-based document analysis can surface hidden verification, test, and documentation debt early in the V-model lifecycle. \textbar\textbar{} TL;DR: This presentation presents ATLAS, an LLM-powered system that identifies, classifies, and prioritizes technical debt in aerospace T\&E artifacts, achieving large time savings and earlier risk mitigation through human-in-the-loop validation. \textbar\textbar{} Contributions: - Defines three categories of T\&E technical debt---verification, test, and documentation---and shows how they compound across the V-model (pp. 8--9). - Introduces the Technical Debt Management Framework (TDMF) integrating preprocessing, LLM analysis, expert validation, and decision support (pp. 7, 9). - Presents ATLAS, an LLM-powered system that parses verification and test artifacts and exports results into SE tools such as DOORS and JIRA (pp. 6, 12). - Reports quantitative gains including  45\% time reduction versus manual review and 87\% expert validation agreement (pp. 10--11). - Demonstrates alignment with DoD Digital Engineering strategy and NASA NPR 7123.1B for lifecycle integration (p. 12). \textbar\textbar{} Challenges: - Calibration and generalization across programs with different data standards. - Dependence on expert availability for validation and governance. - Managing false positives and auditability of AI-generated debt assessments. - Scaling real-time monitoring and multimodal analysis in operational environments.
\par
