  year = 2025,
  month = sep,
  howpublished = {AI4SE \& SE4AI Research and Application Workshop 2025 presentation},
  note = {Insights: Demonstrates that mission engineering (ME) can be significantly accelerated and improved by combining large language models with RAG and GraphRAG techniques to interrogate complex SysML and AFSIM models that are otherwise costly and time-consuming to analyze manually. \textbar\textbar{} TL;DR: This presentation shows how AI-enabled model interrogation enables mission engineers to ask natural-language questions of large, complex mission architectures and simulations, reducing analysis timelines from weeks to interactive time scales. \textbar\textbar{} Contributions: - Identifies the core DoD mission engineering bottleneck: large, complex digital models that are difficult to review and extract insights from despite advances in digital engineering (pp. 3--5). - Proposes an AI-enabled ME workbench that loads SysML and AFSIM models into a conversational interrogation environment using RAG and GraphRAG (pp. 6--11). - Defines priority ME use cases including model content discovery, consistency checking across architecture and simulation models, and gap identification (pp. 9, 17). - Describes a mission engineering model interrogation testbed with curated corpora, automated test questions, and human-subject evaluation metrics (pp. 10--12). - Reports early findings that graph-based representations outperform native tools for analysis and that ME data sparsity and proprietary formats pose major challenges (pp. 13--14). \textbar\textbar{} Challenges: - Mission engineering data lacks standardization and contains sparse relational semantics. - Proprietary vendor formats complicate data extraction and preprocessing. - AI models are not trained on defense-specific ME data, limiting out-of-the-box performance. - Data sensitivity constrains corpus size, model choice, and computational resources.
\par
The MITRE Corporation. Approved for Public Release; Distribution Unlimited. Public Release Case Number 25-2381. Sponsored by OUSD, CAPE, and DoD mission integration stakeholders},
