  note = {Insights: Provides one of the most rigorous evaluations of LLM-assisted requirements generation by grounding performance assessment directly in INCOSE requirements-writing guidelines rather than generic NLP metrics. \textbar\textbar{} TL;DR: This paper shows that LLM-based tools can generate and cluster requirements with coverage and quality comparable to human engineers when evaluated using INCOSE-aligned criteria, though human review remains essential. \textbar\textbar{} Contributions: - Develops a modular test platform supporting requirements extraction from text, images, and subsequent clustering (pp. 1--3). - Decomposes requirements generation into evaluable subtasks to enable controlled experimentation (p. 2). - Proposes an evaluation framework derived from INCOSE requirements-writing best practices, covering coverage, quality, and usability (pp. 3--4; Appendix C). - Demonstrates high quantitative performance for text-based extraction (precision 0.897, recall 0.976) with quality on par with SME-written requirements (pp. 4--5). - Shows that image-based extraction captures high-level features not always explicit in text, highlighting the value of multimodal inputs (p. 4). \textbar\textbar{} Challenges: - Image-based extraction tends to produce less atomic requirements than text-based extraction. - Requirements clustering occasionally groups semantically unrelated themes. - Ambiguous requirements may belong to multiple clusters, limiting fully automated classification. - Usability gains are partial; human-in-the-loop review is still required for validation and refinement.},
